finding: `examples/loading-screen/src/main.ts:186-206` performs a PNG `canvas.toDataURL()` on every animation frame, and before reveal also creates an Image, OffscreenCanvas and pixel buffer per frame (`:94-126`). Capture remains unconditional after `observation.revealed` becomes true. This adds sustained main-thread readback/encoding/allocation to ordinary play, contrary to Shallot's no-steady-allocation rule; capture only once to establish readiness, then keep rendering without readback. It also implements its own pixel capture instead of the public `captureFrame` contract (`src/harness/capture.ts:78-105`), both here and in the browser check (`examples/loading-screen/src/loading-screen.test.ts:78-99`), bypassing the declared common capture geometry/semantics. The retained browser check is present, but its claimed pre-reveal proof is not independently observed: it trusts `frameReadyWithPoster` / `pixelsBeforeReveal` set by the application in the same code path that decides to hide the poster (`main.ts:195-203`), while its separate pixel read is only after reveal (`loading-screen.test.ts:78-120`). A regression in the application's readiness report can therefore self-certify the critical transition. Use the public capture contract to observe the canvas independently while the poster is still visible, then verify host reveal. The current test also does not establish deterministic/stepped behavior; it drives a wall-clock RAF loop and waits on a live browser build. The simple scene/page and actual build-then-frame handoff correct the prior intent failure, and the focus checks/sweep are reported green. However, the required browser integration was not executed past capability admission and there is no new preview, so neither that check nor the visual stopping point is qualified here. This is unverified evidence, not a browser-test product failure; no browser retry was attempted. The revised Check's substantive requirements are appropriate, but its test must independently observe the readiness boundary rather than rely on application-authored success flags.
